dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi Beverly, how are you doing today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm doing well, thank you.

[doctor]: Great to hear. So, you're here for your annual exam. How have you been feeling overall?

[patient]: I've been feeling good, but I have been having some issues with my diabetes lately.

[doctor]: Hmm, okay. Can you tell me more about what's been going on with your diabetes?

[patient]: Sure. I've noticed my blood sugars have been all over the place recently. I think it's because of my new job at the bakery. I've been eating more sweets because of it.

[doctor]: I see. How long have you been working at the bakery?

[patient]: I started this summer.

[doctor]: And how have you been managing your diabetes otherwise? Are you taking your medication regularly?

[patient]: Yes, I take my metformin twice a day and I haven't missed any doses.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. How about your blood pressure? Have you noticed any changes there?

[patient]: My blood pressure has been good, thankfully.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. Are you still taking your lisinopril?

[patient]: Yes, I take it once a day every day.

[doctor]: Excellent. How have you been sleeping? Are you getting enough rest?

[patient]: Yes, I sleep about 8 hours a night and I feel rested.

[doctor]: That's great. Have you had any thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations recently?

[patient]: No, none at all.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. Let's move on to the physical exam. I'll start with your neurological and psychological status. Can you squeeze my hands as hard as you can?

[patient]: Sure. (squeezes hands)

[doctor]: Great, your grip strength is equal bilaterally. Now, let me check your neck. (palpates neck) Your neck is supple without any swelling or lumps. No carotid bruits are appreciable. Now, I'll listen to your lungs. Take a deep breath for me. (listens to lungs) Your lungs are clear without any wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.

[patient]: That's good to know.

[doctor]: Now, I'll check your heart. (listens to heart) Your heart rate is regular, but I do hear a grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. There are no gallops or rubs, and your pulses are good.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: I see that your recent hemoglobin A1c was elevated. This indicates that your diabetes is not well controlled at the moment.

[patient]: Oh, I see. What should I do about that?

[doctor]: We'll continue with your metformin twice a day. I also encourage you to check your fasting blood glucose levels each morning and call them in about 2 weeks. We will discuss further treatment recommendations during your follow-up in about 2 months.

[patient]: Okay, I can do that.

[doctor]: Your blood pressure seems to be relatively stable, so continue taking your lisinopril 10 mg once daily.

[patient]: Got it.

[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think that covers everything.

[doctor]: Alright, Beverly. It was good to see you today. We'll see you in about 2 months for your follow-up.

[patient]: Thank you, doctor.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care! }}<|eot_id|>
