dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi Anna, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, Doctor. I'm okay, but my left elbow has been really bothering me.

[doctor] I see. Can you tell me more about the pain in your elbow?

[patient] Sure. I sit at my desk all day for work with my elbows bent. After work, it's really hard for me to flex and extend my left elbow. It feels really stiff and painful.

[doctor] Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. Have you noticed any swelling in the area?

[patient] Yes, there's some swelling on the back of my elbow, around the olecranon.

[doctor] Alright. Are you experiencing any numbness or tingling in your hands?

[patient] No, no numbness or tingling.

[doctor] How about weakness in your arm?

[patient] I do have some weakness, but it's not too significant.

[doctor] Okay. Any fever or chills?

[patient] No, none of those.

[doctor] Alright. How about your epilepsy? Any recent seizures?

[patient] No, I haven't had any recent seizures. My last one was a year ago.

[doctor] Good to hear. Are you still taking Keppra?

[patient] Yes, I am.

[doctor] And how about your depression? How have you been feeling?

[patient] I've been doing okay with therapy. It's been helpful, but I still feel down and stressed sometimes.

[doctor] That's understandable. Any thoughts of harming yourself or others?

[patient] No, nothing like that.

[doctor] Good. And your peptic ulcer, how has that been?

[patient] I had a lot of stress last year, but an endoscopy showed I had gastritis. I was diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease and started taking Protonix. Everything has been fine since then.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Any chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting?

[patient] No, none of those.

[doctor] You mentioned some medial nasal congestion from allergies, correct?

[patient] Yes, that's right.

[doctor] Alright, let's move on to the physical exam. I'll take a look at your left elbow. Can you show me where it hurts the most?

[patient] It's right here, on the back of my elbow.

[doctor] I see some erythema and edema here. Does it hurt when I press on this area?

[patient] Yes, it does.

[doctor] It's warm to the touch as well. How about when you try to flex and extend your elbow?

[patient] Yes, it hurts when I try to do that.

[doctor] I can feel your radial pulse here. Let's review your recent test results. Your left arm x-ray does not reveal any evidence of fracture. Your EGD showed an episode of gastritis.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Based on your symptoms and exam, I believe you have left olecranon bursitis caused by repetitive trauma. I'll prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once daily for this.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] I also recommend using a pillow to rest your arm on and icing the area to reduce swelling.

[patient] Got it.

[doctor] For your epilepsy, continue taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day. I'll refill your prescription today.

[patient] Thank you.

[doctor] And for your depression, I'll start you on Lexapro 10 mg once daily. How does that sound?

[patient] That sounds good.

[doctor] For your peptic ulcer, continue taking Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think that covers everything.

[doctor] Great. We'll follow up in 2 weeks to see how you're doing with the new medication for your elbow.

[patient] Sounds good. Thank you, Doctor.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care, Anna. }}<|eot_id|>
