dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Good morning, Christine. How are you doing today?

[patient] Good morning, doctor. I'm doing well, thank you. I'm here for my annual exam.

[doctor] Great. Let's get started. How have you been feeling overall?

[patient] I've been feeling pretty good. I have a history of high blood pressure and depression, but I've been managing those well.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Are you still taking your medication for depression?

[patient] Yes, I am. I've been taking it as prescribed and I've also been going to therapy every week. It's been helping a lot.

[doctor] Excellent. How about your sleep? Are you getting enough rest?

[patient] Yes, I sleep well. I get about 8 hours every night.

[doctor] That's great. Any thoughts of self-harm or harming others?

[patient] No, none at all.

[doctor] Good to hear. How about your blood pressure? Have you been checking it at home?

[patient] Yes, I have. It's been fluctuating lately. I haven't had a normal reading in a couple of weeks.

[doctor] Hmm, okay. Have you been taking your blood pressure medications regularly?

[patient] Yes, I have.

[doctor] Alright. How's your diet been?

[patient] It's been a little all over the place. I've been working more hours, and it's been hard to keep up with meals.

[doctor] I understand. Have you been experiencing any other symptoms, like headaches?

[patient] Yes, I've had some headaches, but I thought they were related to my increased screen time.

[doctor] That could be a factor. How's your right hand? Any issues since your carpal tunnel release surgery?

[patient] No, it's been doing well. No numbness or tingling in my hands or fingers.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Let's go over your review of systems. You mentioned blood pressure issues and headaches. Anything else?

[patient] No, that's about it.

[doctor] Alright, let's move on to the physical exam. I'll start with your neurological and psychological status. Your mood and affect seem appropriate. Your grip strength is equal bilaterally, and you're neurologically intact.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Your neck is supple without thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy. Your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. Your heart has a regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs, and no extra heart sounds.

[patient] That's good to know.

[doctor] Your blood pressure has been elevated. We'll need to address that. I'll also review your lab results, which are all within normal limits.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] For your depression, it sounds like you're doing well with your medication and therapy. We'll continue your current regimen.

[patient] That sounds good.

[doctor] For your hypertension, your blood pressures have been fluctuating. You mentioned dietary indiscretion due to increased workload. I'm going to increase your lisinopril to 10 mg once daily.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] Please continue to monitor your blood pressure at home and follow up with me in a month.

[patient] Will do.

[doctor] Your carpal tunnel release is doing well 2 years postoperatively, and you're asymptomatic at this time. If your symptoms return, please contact us.

[patient] I will.

[doctor] Do you have any questions about the plan?

[patient] No, I understand everything.

[doctor] Great. We'll see you in a month for your follow-up. Take care, Christine.

[patient] Thank you, doctor. See you next time.

[doctor] You're welcome. Have a good day.

[patient] You too. Goodbye.

[doctor] Goodbye. }}<|eot_id|>
