dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi Emily, it's good to see you again. How have you been doing since your last visit in 2020?

[patient]: Hi, Doctor. I've been doing alright. I had some insurance changes, so I was switched from Symbicort to Wixela.

[doctor]: Okay, I see. How has Wixela been working for you?

[patient]: It's been working pretty well. I use it once daily, three times a week, and on the other days I use it twice a day as prescribed.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. Are you still using Zyrtec for your seasonal allergies?

[patient]: Yes, I use it in the spring, summer, and fall. It works well for me.

[doctor]: Great. And how about your food allergies? Any issues with peanuts or tree nuts?

[patient]: I try to avoid peanuts and tree nuts. I had an incident last year where I developed hives from cleaning a rental car that had peanuts. But I’ve been careful since then.

[doctor]: Yes, we discussed that before. You were exposed to peanuts when you were cleaning an Enterprise rental car. You developed contact hives and had to use your EpiPen. Have you had any other accidental exposures to peanuts recently?

[patient]: No, I’ve been really careful. I wear long sleeves and change my gloves between each cleaning job. I think it’s likely I’ve been in contact with cars that had peanuts, but I haven’t touched them directly.

[doctor]: That’s good to hear. You mentioned you also avoid tree nuts, right?

[patient]: Yes, I do. I tried almonds and cashew and they were fine, but I still avoid all tree nuts.

[doctor]: That’s wise. Have you had any issues with your asthma recently? Any urgent care or emergency room visits?

[patient]: No, I haven’t had to go to urgent care or the ER. I use my albuterol before I go for a run, but otherwise, I manage without it.

[doctor]: That’s good to hear. I see you’re trying to join the military. How’s that going?

[patient]: It’s going well. I’m hoping to enlist early in the spring of 2022. They require a certain fitness level without the use of a rescue or maintenance inhaler, so I’ve been weaning off my inhalers.

[doctor]: That’s great. Have you been able to meet the physical fitness requirements without your inhaler?

[patient]: Yes, I’ve been managing. I’m still using it occasionally, but mostly I’m fine.

[doctor]: Excellent. I see you’re also trying to quit smoking and vaping. How’s that going?

[patient]: I’ve cut down significantly. I’m trying to quit completely in preparation for the military.

[doctor]: That’s great to hear. Keep up the good work. Have you been using your nebulizer?

[patient]: No, I haven’t needed it recently. I only used it as a child a few times for upper respiratory infections.

[doctor]: Alright. Let’s go over your current medications. You’re using Wixela 250 mcg-50 mcg/dose powder for inhalation, 1 puff twice daily in the morning and evening, correct?

[patient]: Yes, that’s right.

[doctor]: And you’re also using Proair HFA as needed, and you carry an EpiPen for emergencies. Is that correct?

[patient]: Yes, that’s correct.

[doctor]: And you’ve been reducing your Wixela dose to 1 inhalation once daily 3 times per week, with the remaining days using it twice per day as prescribed. How’s that working for you?

[patient]: It’s working well. I feel I don’t always need the full dose.

[doctor]: Good to hear. Your Alvesco 80 mcg, 2 puff 2 times daily, is that still working for you?

[patient]: Yes, it’s fine. I’m using it more consistently now.

[doctor]: Excellent. I see your Asthma Control Test (ACT) score is 21, which is moderate persistent asthma. How do you feel about your asthma management overall?

[patient]: I feel pretty good about it. I think I’m managing it well.

[doctor]: That’s great. Your spirometry test today was better than it was in 2019. You mentioned it was smoky during your last test. Is that right?

[patient]: Yes, it was smoky from wildfires last time.

[doctor]: Understood. For your allergic rhinitis, you’ll continue to use Zyrtec as needed, primarily during spring and fall and sometimes in summer. How’s that working for you?

[patient]: It’s working well. I take it 3 days a week when I work outside.

[doctor]: Perfect. Your symptoms are under control, correct?

[patient]: Yes, they are.

[doctor]: For your peanut allergy, continue to avoid exposure and keep your EpiPen with you at all times. Have you had any close calls recently?

[patient]: No, I’ve been very careful.

[doctor]: That’s good to hear. We’ll refill your EpiPen today since it expires annually. Do you have any questions or concerns about your current treatment plan?

[patient]: No, I think everything sounds good.

[doctor]: Alright. For your asthma, I recommend starting Alvesco 2 puffs twice daily. Use Wixela only if Alvesco doesn’t work well. Continue using albuterol as needed. We’ll repeat ACT and spirometry testing in 4 months. Does that sound good?

[patient]: Yes, that sounds good.

[doctor]: For your allergic rhinitis, continue with Zyrtec 10 mg daily as needed. And for your peanut allergy, continue strict avoidance and keep your EpiPen with you always. We’ll refill it today. Any other questions?

[patient]: No, that’s all.

[doctor]: Great. We’ll see you back in 4 months to repeat the ACT and spirometry testing. Keep up the good work with your lifestyle changes and medications. You’re doing great.

[patient]: Thank you, Doctor.

[doctor]: You’re welcome. Take care, Emily.

[patient]: Thanks, you too. }}<|eot_id|>
