dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi Jordan, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm okay, I guess. I've been having some issues with my blood pressure and these palpitations.

[doctor] Hmm, I see. Can you tell me more about what's been going on?

[patient] Sure. Well, back in March, I went to the emergency room because I was having these really scary palpitations. They said it was because of a panic attack, but I had had a lot of coffee that morning on an empty stomach. Since then, I've been keeping an eye on my blood pressure at home. It's been between 146 and 155 systolic.

[doctor] Hmm, that's quite high. Have you had any more episodes of palpitations since then?

[patient] No, not really. Just the one time in March.

[doctor] Okay, that's good to know. How about your family history? Do any of your family members have hypertension?

[patient] Yes, several of them do.

[doctor] Alright. And what about your current medications? Are you still taking Benicar HCT and Amlodipine?

[patient] Yes, I started with my new primary care physician, Dr. Smith, on Benicar HCT about a month ago. I also still take Amlodipine.

[doctor] Okay, great. Have you noticed any side effects from these medications?

[patient] No, not really. I think I'm doing okay with them.

[doctor] Good to hear. Now, let's talk about your lifestyle. Are you monitoring your salt intake?

[patient] Yes, I've been trying to reduce it. I think I'm doing a good job.

[doctor] That's excellent. And how about exercise? Are you able to stay active?

[patient] I walk on my treadmill as often as I can.

[doctor] That's great. Now, let's move on to your physical exam. Your cardiovascular exam shows a normal rate and regular rhythm, with normal heart sounds and pulses. Your breath sounds are normal, and there's no tenderness in your abdomen or edema in your limbs. 

[patient] Okay, that's good to know.

[doctor] I also reviewed your thyroid profile from March 1st, 2021. Everything looks normal there. Now, based on your blood pressure readings and the fact that you're on maximal doses of three medications, it looks like you have resistant hypertension.

[patient] Oh, what does that mean?

[doctor] Resistant hypertension means that your blood pressure is not adequately controlled with the current medications. To address this, I'm going to add spironolactone to your regimen. This will require monitoring of your potassium and sodium levels.

[patient] Okay, I understand.

[doctor] We'll also need to schedule a follow-up in two weeks to complete a basic metabolic panel and check your blood pressure. If we're not able to improve your blood pressure with spironolactone, we may need to refer you to a resistant hypertension clinic.

[patient] Alright, I can do that.

[doctor] I also want to counsel you about the potential side effects of spironolactone, including gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. You should also watch out for signs of dehydration, like dizziness.

[patient] Got it. Anything else I should know?

[doctor] Yes, I recommend you visit the American Heart Association website at heart.org for tips on lowering your salt intake and increasing your potassium intake. Your maximum salt intake should be 1500 to <2000 mg per day. I'll also provide you with materials to follow the DASH diet and encourage you to meet 30 minutes of walking most days.

[patient] Okay, I can do that.

[doctor] Great. So, to summarize, we'll add spironolactone to your regimen, monitor your potassium and sodium, and follow up in two weeks for a blood pressure check and basic metabolic panel. Reduce your sodium intake to 1500 to <2000 mg per day and follow the DASH diet. Do you have any questions?

[patient] No, I think that's everything. Thank you, doctor.

[doctor] You're welcome, Jordan. Take care and see you in two weeks.

[patient] Thank you, see you then. }}<|eot_id|>
