dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Good morning, Mr. Watson. How are you feeling today?

[patient]: Good morning, Doctor. I'm doing okay, thank you.

[doctor]: I'm glad to hear that. I see you're here for a follow-up on your hydrocephalus. How have you been since your shunt surgery?

[patient]: Well, I've been better. I still have some issues, but my daughter here can tell you more.

[doctor]: Of course. Hi, Theresa. How has Mr. Watson been doing since his surgery?

[theresa]: Hi, Doctor. Since the surgery, he can walk to the dining room without any trouble, which he couldn't do before. His balance has improved too, but he still has some walking issues. He’s also improved a bit with his bladder control, but he still wears a diaper.

[doctor]: I see. That’s good to hear about the improvement in walking. Has he had any headaches or pain at the shunt site or in the abdomen?

[theresa]: No, no headaches or pain at all.

[doctor]: That’s good. How about his cognitive abilities? Any changes there?

[theresa]: Yes, there’s been some improvement. I feel like I can have normal conversations with him again, which was a big change from before.

[doctor]: That’s excellent news. Let’s review his past history. He has a history of urinary incontinence, adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, and cognitive impairment, correct?

[theresa]: Yes, that’s right.

[doctor]: Alright, let’s go over his review of systems. Has he had any fever or unintentional weight loss?

[theresa]: No, none of that.

[doctor]: Any issues with genital sores, decreased or painful output?

[theresa]: No, nothing like that.

[doctor]: How about headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations?

[theresa]: No, he hasn’t had any of those.

[doctor]: And no depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings?

[theresa]: No, none of those either.

[doctor]: Great. Let’s check his vitals. His blood pressure is 124/80, heart rate is 64, respiratory rate is 18, weight is 174 pounds, and his pain is 0/10. All of these look good.

[theresa]: Yes, he’s been feeling pretty good overall.

[doctor]: Now, let’s do a physical exam. I see the shunt site is clean, dry, and intact at a setting of 2.0. How are you feeling, Mr. Watson?

[patient]: I feel alright, Doctor.

[doctor]: Let’s test your mental status. Can you remember these three words for me? Apple, table, and penny?

[patient]: Apple and table, I remember those. The third one is a bit fuzzy.

[doctor]: That’s alright. You got two out of three. Now, let’s test your calculations. What is 17 multiplied by 3?

[patient]: Hmm, that would be 51.

[doctor]: Correct. Now, let’s check your gait. I’ll use the Tinetti assessment tool. Can you walk for me?

[patient]: Sure.

[doctor]: (Observing) His gait score is 6-8/12 and his balance score is 12/16 for a total score of 18-20/28. This is a slight improvement from his initial score of 15-17/28. Now, let’s check your cranial nerves. Pupils are equal, extraocular movements are intact, face is symmetric, and no dysarthria. Motor strength is normal. Coordination is slow for finger-to-nose.

[theresa]: He’s been working on his coordination.

[doctor]: Yes, I see. Now, let’s review the CT scan results from 10/15/2020. The scan shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Munro of 4.6 cm with a 3rd ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. The radiologist didn’t notice this, and there are also substantial small vessel ischemic changes.

[theresa]: Is that bad?

[doctor]: It’s not something to worry about right now. The tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region isn’t symptomatic and wasn’t documented by the radiologist. Mr. Watson has made some improvement with his hydrocephalus since the shunt surgery. I don’t think he will be able to drive again due to his slow movements, but he can always enroll in a driver’s safety program if he wants.

[patient]: I understand. I was hoping to drive again.

[doctor]: I know, Mr. Watson. It’s just that his speed of movement isn’t sufficient for driving safely. We’ll follow up in 3 months and see how he’s doing. If there’s any significant change, we’ll reassess.

[patient]: Okay, thank you.

[doctor]: You’re welcome. Do you have any other questions?

[theresa]: No, I think that covers everything.

[doctor]: Alright, we’ll see you in 3 months. Take care, Mr. Watson.

[patient]: Thank you, Doctor.

[theresa]: Thank you, Doctor.

[doctor]: You’re welcome. Goodbye.

[patient]: Goodbye. }}<|eot_id|>
