dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi Jacqueline, how are you today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm doing okay, thank you. I'm here for a follow-up on my perioral dermatitis.

[doctor]: Great, let's talk about how you've been doing since your last visit. How has your perioral dermatitis been?

[patient]: It's been a bit better, but it hasn't fully resolved. It's still there, but it hasn't gotten worse.

[doctor]: I see. You mentioned that you were using doxycycline twice a day. How has that been going?

[patient]: Yes, I was applying it twice a day, but my pharmacist advised me to reduce it to once daily since it had started to improve. So, for about a week now, I've been using it only once a day.

[doctor]: Okay, and have you noticed any changes in your symptoms since you reduced the dosage?

[patient]: Not really. It hasn't gotten worse, but it hasn't improved much either.

[doctor]: Hmm, alright. And you're still using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily?

[patient]: Yes, I am. My skin doesn't feel dry, and it's much better moisturized.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. Are you applying MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash?

[patient]: Yes, I am. I haven't used any other lotions or products like Cetaphil lotion, soap, or Neutrogena wipes.

[doctor]: That's good. You mentioned that you were concerned about using Elidel because of the cost and possible side effects. Is that still the case?

[patient]: Yes, I was worried about the cost and the side effects. I decided not to use it.

[doctor]: Understandable. Let's take a look at your face. I'll perform a skin inspection now. 

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: On inspection, I see a few very faint erythematous papules with mild redness on your bilateral medial cheeks and around and underneath your nostrils. Your skin type is 4 to 5. 

[patient]: What does that mean?

[doctor]: It means you have a moderate skin type. It's prone to dryness and irritation but also has some oiliness. Now, regarding your perioral dermatitis, it seems like reducing the doxycycline might have stalled your progress. I recommend you resume the twice daily application until you see full resolution, and then you can go back to once a day for an additional week.

[patient]: Okay, I can do that.

[doctor]: I also want to discuss the rosacea. You have ocular rosacea as well, correct?

[patient]: Yes, I do.

[doctor]: Continue using MetroCream twice daily. I also recommend gently washing your eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and using a gentle cream like Vanicream.

[patient]: Alright, I can do that.

[doctor]: You should continue with the Sulfacetamide face wash once a day and use a Bruder mask as needed. 

[patient]: Got it.

[doctor]: If your symptoms worsen, please call the clinic. We'll have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks to reassess.

[patient]: Okay, thank you, doctor.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care, Jacqueline.

[patient]: Thank you. Bye.

[doctor]: Bye. }}<|eot_id|>
