dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi Matthew, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, Doctor. I'm doing alright, but my shoulder's been giving me a lot of trouble.

[doctor] I see. Can you tell me more about what happened?

[patient] Sure. I was playing a game of pick-up football about four days ago. I got tackled a couple of times and landed hard on my right shoulder.

[doctor] Ouch, that sounds painful. Did you return to the game after that?

[patient] Yeah, I did, but the pain started after that. It's been pretty bad since then.

[doctor] Hmm, where exactly is the pain located?

[patient] It's on the back of my shoulder, just here.

[doctor] Okay, and have you experienced any numbness or tingling in your fingers?

[patient] No, no numbness or tingling. Just the pain.

[doctor] How about the strength in your hand? Can you still grip things normally?

[patient] The strength is okay, but I've been avoiding lifting anything heavy because it hurts.

[doctor] I understand. Can you touch your shoulder to your ear?

[patient] No, I can't. It hurts too much.

[doctor] Got it. Have you had any neck problems or other joint issues recently?

[patient] No, no neck problems or other joint issues.

[doctor] Any previous injuries to your right shoulder?

[patient] No, this is the first time I've had any problems with it.

[doctor] Any fever or chills since the injury?

[patient] No, but I did get a headache.

[doctor] Did you hit your head when you fell?

[patient] Yeah, I hit my head, but I didn't pass out.

[doctor] Alright. Let's talk a bit about your medical history. Have you had any surgeries in the past?

[patient] Yes, I had an appendectomy about three to four years ago.

[doctor] Got it. Now, let's review some of your systems. Any fever or chills?

[patient] No, none.

[doctor] Any other joint pain besides your shoulder?

[patient] No, just the shoulder.

[doctor] How about headaches? You mentioned you had one.

[patient] Yes, I've had a headache since the injury.

[doctor] Any numbness or tingling in your fingers?

[patient] No, none.

[doctor] Okay, let's take a look at your vitals. Your blood pressure is 122/76, heart rate is 82 beats per minute, and your oxygen saturation is 95% on room air. Everything looks good there.

[patient] That's good to hear.

[doctor] Let's move on to the physical exam. I'll start with your neck. Any tenderness here?

[patient] No, no tenderness.

[doctor] Full range of motion, good. Now, let's check your head. No trauma, no bruising or swelling. Now, let's examine your right shoulder. Does it hurt when I press here?

[patient] Yes, that's painful.

[doctor] Okay, pain on the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. How about when you move your arm like this?

[patient] Yes, that hurts too.

[doctor] Decreased abduction and adduction to about 90 degrees, pain with supination and pronation. Your radial artery pulse is palpable. 

[patient] What does that mean?

[doctor] It means that there is pain when you move your arm in certain directions, but your pulse is normal, which is good. We took an X-ray of your shoulder, and it shows an acute fracture of the acromioclavicular joint.

[patient] Oh, that's not good.

[doctor] It's not ideal, but we have a plan. I'll place you in a sling to support your shoulder. I'll also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day to help with the pain.

[patient] Okay, what else should I do?

[doctor] We should get a CT scan of your right shoulder to get a better look at the extent of the fracture. I also recommend formal physical therapy to strengthen your shoulder. This will help in the long run and might avoid the need for surgery.

[patient] That sounds like a lot.

[doctor] I understand it can be overwhelming. It's important to avoid tackle football for now to let your shoulder heal.

[patient] Alright, I can do that.

[doctor] Do you have any questions about the plan?

[patient] No, I think I understand. 

[doctor] Great. We'll get you set up with the sling and the medication. I'll also schedule the CT scan and physical therapy appointments. 

[patient] Thank you, Doctor.

[doctor] You're welcome, Matthew. Take care and follow the instructions closely. 

[patient] Will do. Thanks again.

[doctor] Take care, Matthew. See you soon.

[patient] Bye. }}<|eot_id|>
