dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi Jeremy, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, Doctor. I've been better, to be honest. My back has been really bothering me.

[doctor] I'm sorry to hear that. Can you tell me more about your back pain?

[patient] Sure. The pain started about 4 months ago when I was moving some boxes out of my son's college dorm. I felt this tingling in my right toes, but it went away after about an hour. Since then, I've had this sharp pain in my lower back, especially when I turn a certain way or roll over in my sleep. It gets better when I reposition myself.

[doctor] I see. And how would you describe the pain? Is it constant or does it come and go?

[patient] It's mostly constant, but it gets worse with certain movements. It's really affecting my sleep and daily activities.

[doctor] That sounds uncomfortable. Have you tried any treatments for the pain?

[patient] Yes, I've been taking Tylenol and ibuprofen. Heat and ice also help a bit, but not much. Ibuprofen seems to be slightly more effective than Tylenol.

[doctor] Okay, and have you noticed any other symptoms like upset stomach from the ibuprofen?

[patient] No, no upset stomach. It’s just the back pain.

[doctor] Alright. Have you tried any physical therapy?

[patient] Yes, I tried some physical therapy at my gym, but it didn't really help.

[doctor] I see. Any issues with bladder or bowel movements, fevers, or chills?

[patient] No, none of those.

[doctor] Okay, let's review your medical history. Do you have any other medical conditions or surgeries?

[patient] Yes, I had a right knee replacement in the spring of 2020. My knee feels much better now, about 95% improved.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Now, let's move on to a physical examination. I'll start with checking your neck and back. Please let me know if you feel any pain.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] (Examining) I notice a bony protuberance at the C5-C6 level, but no pain to palpation there. Now, let's check your lumbar spine. (Examining) No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine, but I see decreased flexion and extension of your lower back. Does this hurt?

[patient] Yes, a bit.

[doctor] Alright, I also notice a positive straight leg raise and spasm of the paraspinal muscles in your lumbar spine. Your strength is equal bilaterally. I'll also check your vitals. Your temperature is normal, blood pressure is 117/56 mm Hg, heart rate is 68 BPM, and oxygenation is 95% on room air. Everything looks stable.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] We took X-rays of your lumbar spine, and they didn't show any acute bony abnormalities. However, I recommend getting an MRI to evaluate for a possible acute disc herniation. 

[patient] Alright, I can do that.

[doctor] In the meantime, I'll prescribe meloxicam 15 mg for your pain. Continue with physical therapy, and I'll provide a referral for that today. We'll also schedule the MRI to get a clearer picture of what's going on.

[patient] Thank you, Doctor. I appreciate it.

[doctor] You're welcome, Jeremy. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think that covers everything.

[doctor] Great. We'll get you set up for the MRI and the referral for physical therapy. Take care and we'll follow up once we have the MRI results.

[patient] Thanks, Doctor. I'll see you soon.

[doctor] Take care, Jeremy. Bye now.

[patient] Bye. }}<|eot_id|>
