[patient]: That's good to hear.

[doctor]: Now, let's move on to the physical exam. I'll start with your neck. Your neck is supple, no jugular venous distension, and no carotid bruits. Everything looks normal there.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: I'll listen to your lungs now. I hear some rales and rhonchi, more on the right side. There are diminished breath sounds on the right as well, but no wheezing right now.

[patient]: What does that mean?

[doctor]: It means there are some abnormal sounds in your lungs, which could be due to an infection or other issues. We'll need to investigate further.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: I'll listen to your heart now. Your heart has a regular rate and rhythm, but I do hear a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. This hasn't changed from before.

[patient]: What does that mean?

[doctor]: It's a type of heart murmur that we've noted before. It's not necessarily new, but we'll keep an eye on it.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: I'll check your abdomen now. Your abdomen is soft, which is good.

[patient]: That's good to hear.

[doctor]: I'll examine your legs now. I see you have 1+ nonpitting edema on both lower extremities, but no calf tenderness. Also, no Homan's sign, which is good.

[patient]: What does the edema mean?

[doctor]: It means there's some swelling, but it's not severe and doesn't indicate deep vein thrombosis.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Lastly, I don't see any bruising on your body, which is good.

[patient]: That's a relief.

[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and exam, we need to do some further testing. I'm ordering a chest X-ray and a CT scan, along with some blood work. I want you to go to the ER right away for these tests.

[patient]: The ER? Is it that serious?

[doctor]: It's important to rule out any serious conditions like pneumonia or something more serious. I'll call ahead to the ER to let them know you're coming.

[patient]: Alright, I understand.

[doctor]: Now, regarding your diabetes, your blood sugar hasn't been well controlled. I'm ordering an updated A1c test today. I also want you to increase your metformin to 750 mg twice daily.

[patient]: Okay, I can do that.

[doctor]: For your hypertension, your blood pressure has been running low for about three weeks. I think this could be due to your shortness of breath. We need to understand the cause of your shortness of breath before we proceed with your hypertension treatment. For now, I want you to stop taking Norvasc.

[patient]: Alright, I'll stop taking it.

[doctor]: We'll have you follow up in about three weeks to discuss your diabetes and further treatment recommendations. Do you have any questions?

[patient]: No, I think I understand everything.

[doctor]: Great. If you experience any worsening symptoms, don't hesitate to go to the ER immediately.

[patient]: I will. Thank you, Doctor.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care and see you in three weeks.

[patient]: Thank you, Doctor. }}<|eot_id|>
