dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi Scott, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm doing okay, thanks. How about you?

[doctor] I'm doing well, thank you. So, I see you're here for a right knee injury. Can you tell me a bit more about what happened?

[patient] Sure, it happened a few days ago. I was playing in a pickup soccer game and I felt a pop in my right knee. It was pretty painful and swollen right after.

[doctor] I see. How has it been since then? Has the swelling improved at all?

[patient] Yes, the swelling has gone down a bit, but my knee still isn't moving as well as it used to. It hurts quite a bit, especially when I try to straighten it.

[doctor] Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. On a scale of 1 to 10, how would you rate your pain right now?

[patient] I'd say it's about a 6 or 7 out of 10.

[doctor] And how about when the swelling is less? Does the pain change then?

[patient] Yeah, when the swelling goes down, the pain goes down too. It's more like a 4 to 5 out of 10 then.

[doctor] Okay, that's helpful to know. Have you been taking anything for the pain?

[patient] Yes, I've been taking ibuprofen regularly, and it helps a bit.

[doctor] Good to know. Can you bend your knee for me?

[patient] Sure, but it hurts towards the end of the motion.

[doctor] I see. And how about when you try to straighten it?

[patient] It feels like I have to help it straighten. It doesn't go all the way on its own.

[doctor] Okay, and are you able to bear weight on it?

[patient] Yes, I can, but it feels loose and not normal.

[doctor] Alright. Let's talk a bit about your lifestyle. What do you do for work?

[patient] I work at a desk.

[doctor] Got it. Do you play soccer regularly?

[patient] Yes, I do. I play in a few leagues and also pickup games.

[doctor] Understood. Now, let's do a physical exam. I'll need you to walk for me. 

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] I see you have a marked limp in your right leg when you walk. Let's take a closer look at your knee. I'll start with examining the right knee. There is significant effusion and slight ecchymosis. Does it hurt when I press here?

[patient] Yes, it does.

[doctor] How about here?

[patient] Yes, that hurts too.

[doctor] Okay, I see there's pain with palpation at the medial joint line. Let's check your range of motion. Can you bend your knee for me?

[patient] Sure, but it hurts towards the end.

[doctor] I can see that. You lack a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees and lacking 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion due to pain. Let's do a Lachman's test. I'll move your knee like this. Does that hurt?

[patient] Yes, it does.

[doctor] Alright, that's a positive Lachman's test. How about muscle strength? Can you push against my hand?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] Good, your muscle strength is 5/5. Now, let's check your neurovascular status. Do you feel this?

[patient] Yes, I can feel that.

[doctor] Great, your neurovascular status is intact distally. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, I'm concerned you might have an anterior cruciate ligament tear. We need to get an MRI of your right knee to confirm this.

[patient] Okay, that makes sense.

[doctor] In the meantime, I'll prescribe meloxicam 15 mg a day to help with the swelling and pain. Make sure to use ice and modify your activity to reduce swelling and pain. Wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches when you're walking. Avoid jogging until we get the MRI results.

[patient] Got it. How long will it take to get the MRI results?

[doctor] It should be ready within a few days. We'll schedule a follow-up appointment once we have the results for further discussion.

[patient] Alright, thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. Do you have any other questions for me?

[patient] No, I think that's it for now.

[doctor] Okay, take care and we'll see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
