dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi Gregory, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm doing okay, but I've been having some trouble with my right knee.

[doctor] Hmm, I see. Can you tell me a bit more about what's been going on with your knee?

[patient] Sure. It started about 2.5 weeks ago when I stepped off a curb and twisted my ankle. Since then, I've had pain on the outside of my right knee.

[doctor] I see. And how would you describe the pain? Is it constant or does it come and go?

[patient] It's mostly there, but it gets worse if I stand on one foot for a long time or if my knee locks up when I stand for a while.

[doctor] Hmm, okay. Do you feel any pain when your knee is at rest, or when you're sitting or lying down?

[patient] No, it doesn't hurt when I'm resting or when my foot is flat on the ground.

[doctor] Alright. Do you notice any clicking or other sounds when you walk?

[patient] Yes, there's definitely some clicking when I walk.

[doctor] Got it. How about numbness or tingling in your right foot? Have you experienced that before?

[patient] Yes, I've had numbness and tingling in my right foot before, but it's not new.

[doctor] Okay, that's important to note. Have you tried any treatments or medications for the pain?

[patient] Yes, I've been taking ibuprofen at night, and it helps. I've also been icing my knee four times a day, and that seems to help in the morning and at the end of the day.

[doctor] That's good to hear that those measures are helping. Can you tell me about your daily activities? Are you able to stay active?

[patient] Yes, I'm more active during the day. I can perform a full squat without pain.

[doctor] That's good to know. Do you have any other medical history we should be aware of?

[patient] Well, I've fractured my second toe three times over the last 10 years.

[doctor] Alright, thanks for sharing that. Now, let's talk a bit about your lifestyle. What do you do for work?

[patient] I work in sales.

[doctor] And do you exercise regularly?

[patient] Yes, I ride a bike for exercise.

[doctor] Great. Now, let's move on to a physical examination. I'll be checking a few things to get a better understanding of your knee. Is that okay?

[patient] Sure, go ahead.

[doctor] Alright, I'll start by checking your pulses. Your dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulses are palpable. Now, I'll examine your right knee. I see there's slight edema and slight effusion. Does it hurt when I press here?

[patient] No, it doesn't hurt when you press there.

[doctor] Okay, good. I'll perform a varus test now. Tell me if you feel any pain.

[patient] Yes, that hurts.

[doctor] Alright, that's a positive varus test. Now, let's check your range of motion. Can you move your knee for me?

[patient] Sure. (Moves knee)

[doctor] Your range of motion is full. Now, I'll do a Lachman's test. (Performs test) That's negative. I also reviewed your X-rays, which show no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormalities.

[patient] That's good to know.

[doctor] Based on your symptoms and the examination, it looks like you have a right knee lateral collateral ligament strain. We'll treat this conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen your knee. I'll also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg to help with the pain.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] If your pain doesn't improve with physical therapy, we may need to get an MRI to further evaluate the ligaments and surrounding structures.

[patient] Alright, I understand.

[doctor] Do you have any questions about the plan?

[patient] No, I think that covers everything.

[doctor] Great. I'll have the nurse come in to schedule your physical therapy sessions and give you the prescription for meloxicam. We'll see you back in 4 weeks to assess your progress.

[patient] Thank you, doctor.

[doctor] You're welcome, Gregory. Take care and see you soon.

[patient] Thanks, you too. }}<|eot_id|>
