dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi Dennis, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, Doctor. I'm doing okay, but I'm here because of this pain in my right heel.

[doctor] I see. Can you tell me more about the pain you're experiencing?

[patient] Sure. The pain started about 10 days ago while I was training for a triathlon. It's on the back of my heel and sometimes it goes up into my calf. It gets worse when I work out and when I get up in the morning. If I sit for a long time and then stand up, it hurts again.

[doctor] Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. How would you describe the pain? Is it sharp, dull, throbbing?

[patient] It's more of a dull ache, but it can get pretty sharp sometimes, especially when I'm working out.

[doctor] And you mentioned it gets worse with exercise. How often do you exercise?

[patient] I run about 10 kilometers every morning, and then at lunch, I try to get on the bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. In the evening, I go to the aquatic center and swim for 30 minutes to an hour.

[doctor] That’s quite a lot of exercise. Do you take any days off from your routine?

[patient] I usually take one day off a week.

[doctor] Okay. Do you stretch before or after your workouts?

[patient] No, I don’t really stretch at all.

[doctor] Alright. Do you have any pain in your left leg?

[patient] No, just the right heel.

[doctor] Got it. Let’s talk a bit about your lifestyle. Do you have any other medical conditions or take any medications?

[patient] No, I don't have any other medical conditions and I'm not on any medications.

[doctor] Okay, let’s move on to the physical exam. I’m going to check your heel and calf. Let me know if you feel any pain. 

[patient] Sure, go ahead.

[doctor] (Examining) I see. You have pain with resisted extension and mild pain with flexion. There is severe tenderness to the Achilles tendon. Let’s do the calcaneal squeeze test. Does this hurt?

[patient] Yes, it does.

[doctor] And the Thompson test is negative on both sides. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, it looks like you have Achilles tendinitis. 

[patient] Oh, I see. What does that mean?

[doctor] Achilles tendinitis is an inflammation of the Achilles tendon, which connects your calf muscles to your heel bone. It’s often caused by overuse, especially in people who do a lot of high-impact activities like running and jumping.

[patient] That makes sense. What can we do about it?

[doctor] First, I recommend that you take at least one week off from your training to allow your body to recover. I’ll also refer you to physical therapy to strengthen your right lower extremities. 

[patient] Okay, I can do that.

[doctor] For pain relief, you can take 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours. It should help reduce the inflammation and pain.

[patient] Alright, I’ll do that.

[doctor] If your pain doesn’t improve with physical therapy, we may need to get an x-ray to rule out any other issues. 

[patient] Got it. How long should I wait before coming back for a follow-up?

[doctor] I’d like to see you back in 7 to 10 days to check on your progress. 

[patient] Okay, I’ll make sure to schedule that.

[doctor] Great. Do you have any other questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think that covers everything. Thank you, Doctor.

[doctor] You’re welcome, Dennis. Take care and I’ll see you in a few days.

[patient] Thanks, Doctor. See you then. }}<|eot_id|>
