dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi Christina, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, doctor. I'm okay, but I'm really worried about this ulcer on my foot.

[doctor] I understand. Let's talk about it. Can you tell me more about when this ulcer started?

[patient] Sure, it started about a year ago. I got a blister on my right foot, and I kept picking at it. It turned red, and it hasn't healed since.

[doctor] Hmm, I see. And you mentioned you were treated for it before?

[patient] Yes, my primary care doctor gave me Keflex, and it did help a bit, but it didn't completely go away. I was on it about 5 months ago.

[doctor] Alright. Are you experiencing any pain with the ulcer?

[patient] No, there's no pain, but I have noticed more drainage and I'm feeling really tired lately.

[doctor] I see. Have you been wearing your regular shoes?

[patient] Yes, I have.

[doctor] Okay. And how is your diabetes management going? How are your blood sugars?

[patient] My blood sugars are better than they used to be, but they're still in the 200's. My last A1C was 10.

[doctor] Alright, thank you for that information. Have you noticed any changes in sensation in your foot?

[patient] Yes, I have decreased sensation in my foot.

[doctor] Okay, thank you. Let me take a look at your foot now. 

[patient] Sure, here it is.

[doctor] Alright, I see a 1.0 cm x 2.0 cm wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. There is some yellow slough and minimal granulation tissue. There is no surrounding erythema or cellulitis. I don't see any fluid collection, necrosis, or bony exposure. Does it hurt when I press here?

[patient] No, it doesn't hurt.

[doctor] Good to know. Let's talk about the next steps. I recommend we do an ankle brachial index to check the blood supply to your foot. We should also do a debridement today to remove any dead tissue.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] I'll also recommend you wear a surgical shoe to provide increased support. I'm going to prescribe collagenase ointment for you to apply to the wound once a day, and cover it with a sterile dressing. This should be continued until we see the ulcer decrease in size.

[patient] Alright, I can do that.

[doctor] Great. We'll follow up in 2 weeks to check on your progress. Do you have any questions for me?

[patient] No, I think that covers everything.

[doctor] Excellent. Thank you for coming in today, Christina. We'll see you in 2 weeks.

[patient] Thank you, doctor. }}<|eot_id|>
