dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you feeling today?

[patient] Hi, I'm doing okay, thank you.

[doctor] Great to hear. I'm Dr. Smith. I see you're here for a follow-up after your recent inferior STEMI. How have you been feeling since then?

[patient] I've been feeling alright, no major issues. Just a bit worried about everything.

[doctor] Understandable. Let's go over your history a bit. You had a stent placed in 2018 for a RCA blockage, correct?

[patient] Yes, that's right.

[doctor] And you're currently taking aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, Toprol 50 mg daily, lisinopril 20 mg daily, and metformin for your diabetes, correct?

[patient] Yes, that's all correct.

[doctor] Good. Have you experienced any chest pain recently?

[patient] No, no chest pain at all.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Any shortness of breath?

[patient] No, I haven't had any shortness of breath either.

[doctor] How about swelling in your legs?

[patient] Yes, I've noticed some swelling in both my legs.

[doctor] Alright, let's check your vitals. Everything looks within normal limits. Let me do a quick physical exam. 

[doctor] I'm going to check your neck first. No jugular venous distention. Now, I'll listen to your lungs. Your lungs are clear bilaterally. 

[doctor] Let's listen to your heart. I hear a 3/6 systolic ejection murmur at the left base. Your radial artery cath site looks clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable. 

[doctor] Now, let's check your legs. I see some bilateral lower limb edema with pitting 1+. 

[doctor] I reviewed your EKG, and it shows a normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression, which is expected after a STEMI. Your echocardiogram shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. 

[doctor] Based on your history and exam, here's what we'll do. First, for your coronary artery disease, you'll continue your current medications: aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, along with Lipitor 80 mg daily and Toprol 50 mg daily. I'll also refer you to cardiac rehab to help you regain confidence and learn how to exercise safely.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] For your reduced left ventricular function and moderate mitral regurgitation, your recent echocardiogram shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. This is likely due to the muscle being "stunned" from your STEMI, but it should improve over time since you're compliant with your medications.

[patient] That's reassuring to hear.

[doctor] Continue taking lisinopril 20 mg daily. Now, regarding your fluid retention, I'm going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day and add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. We'll also do some lab work and a repeat echo in about two months.

[patient] Alright, I'll do that.

[doctor] Your blood pressure is well controlled, so you can continue with lisinopril 20 mg daily. I also encourage you to continue exercising and be mindful of your diet. There are healthier alternatives to reduce the amount of preserved meats. I'll refer you to nutrition counseling to explore these options.

[patient] Okay, I'll look into that.

[doctor] Lastly, for your type 2 diabetes, continue monitoring your blood glucose levels as you have been. 

[patient] Will do.

[doctor] Do you have any questions or concerns about the treatment plan?

[patient] No, everything sounds clear. Thank you.

[doctor] Great. We'll see you back in two months for a follow-up, and we'll repeat the echocardiogram then. If you have any issues before then, don't hesitate to contact us.

[patient] Thank you, Doctor.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care and see you soon.

[patient] Thanks, you too. }}<|eot_id|>
