[doctor] Alright, I'm going to check the range of motion in your wrist. Please try to flex and extend your wrist for me. Does this cause any pain?

[patient] Yes, it hurts when I do that.

[doctor] I see. Now, I'm going to check your grip strength. Can you squeeze my fingers as hard as you can?

[patient] Okay. [squeezes]

[doctor] Thank you. Your grip strength is definitely weaker on your right hand compared to your left. Let's check for Tinel's sign. I'm going to tap on the inside of your wrist. Let me know if you feel any tingling or pain.

[patient] Yes, I feel tingling.

[doctor] That's a positive Tinel's sign. Based on your symptoms and the exam, it looks like you may have carpal tunnel syndrome.

[patient] Oh, what does that mean?

[doctor] Carpal tunnel syndrome is a condition where the median nerve is compressed at the wrist, leading to numbness, tingling, and pain in the hand and fingers. It's common in people who use their hands extensively, especially those who type or use a mouse frequently.

[patient] That makes sense. What can we do about it?

[doctor] We can start with conservative treatments. I recommend using a wrist splint at night to keep your wrist in a neutral position. I also want you to increase your ibuprofen dose to 600 mg, four times a day.

[patient] Okay, I can do that.

[doctor] I also want to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study to evaluate the severity of your carpal tunnel syndrome. This will help us determine if you need additional treatment like physical therapy or, in some cases, surgery.

[patient] Alright, that sounds good.

[doctor] Do you have any questions about the plan?

[patient] No, I think I understand. 

[doctor] Great. If the EMG study comes back positive, we can consider additional treatments like physical therapy or even carpal tunnel release surgery. But let's start with the conservative approach and see how you respond.

[patient] Okay, thank you, doctor.

[doctor] You're welcome. We'll schedule the EMG study and I'll see you back in two weeks to review the results. In the meantime, start using the wrist splint and take the increased dose of ibuprofen.

[patient] Got it. Thank you.

[doctor] Take care, Alexis. See you in two weeks.

[patient] Thanks, doctor. See you then. }}<|eot_id|>
