dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Good morning, Gregory. How are you doing today?

[patient] Good morning, doctor. I've been better, to be honest. My ankle is really bothering me.

[doctor] I'm sorry to hear that. I see you're here for right ankle pain and swelling. Can you tell me more about what happened?

[patient] Sure. I slipped and fell on the ice while taking out the trash yesterday. I couldn't put any weight on it, and a friend had to help me up. Since then, I've been icing and elevating it, and it seems to have improved a bit, but it's still pretty painful.

[doctor] That sounds painful. Did you experience any numbness in your right foot?

[patient] No, there's no numbness. It just hurts a lot.

[doctor] Okay, that's good to know. Have you had any previous injuries to this ankle?

[patient] No, I don't recall any previous injuries to this ankle.

[doctor] Alright. You mentioned you've been icing and elevating your ankle. Have you taken any medication for the pain?

[patient] Yes, I've been taking ibuprofen, and it has helped a bit.

[doctor] That's good to hear. How about your activity level? Are you generally active?

[patient] Yes, I'm very active. I play soccer for the community soccer league.

[doctor] Great, playing soccer. That must be tough with an injured ankle. Let's review your vitals quickly. Your vitals signs look good today. Now, let's move on to the physical exam.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] I see some mild edema in your right ankle. Your sensation is normal. Let me examine your ankle. There is some ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus. Is it tender when I press here?

[patient] Yes, that hurts.

[doctor] Alright, there's tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. How about the bony tenderness on palpation of the foot? Any pain there?

[patient] No, not really.

[doctor] Good. Your neurovascular status is intact. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds, and you have a strong dorsalis pedis pulse. Sensation is intact to light touch. We took three views of your right ankle earlier, and the results came back. There is no evidence of a fracture.

[patient] That's a relief.

[doctor] Yes, it is. Based on your symptoms and the examination, it looks like you have a right ankle sprain. We discussed the treatment options, and I advised you to keep your right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. Continue icing your ankle, and I'll provide you with an Aircast to help stabilize it.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] You should also use crutches for the next 1 to 2 days to avoid putting weight on your ankle. As you start bearing weight, do so as tolerated. For pain control, you can continue taking NSAIDs as needed.

[patient] Got it. How long before I should start seeing improvement?

[doctor] You should see significant improvement in your symptoms over the next 2 weeks. We'll have you follow up with me in 2 weeks to assess your progress and determine your return to play date.

[patient] Alright, I'll make sure to do that.

[doctor] If your symptoms change or worsen before your next appointment, please call the office. Do you have any other questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think that covers it. Thank you, doctor.

[doctor] You're welcome, Gregory. Take care and see you in 2 weeks.

[patient] Thanks, doctor. See you then. }}<|eot_id|>
